title: find -printf is GNU-only, so pidly does not run on macOS
effort: M
area: cli
created: 2026-09-17T07:12:58Z
updated: 2026-09-17T07:12:58Z

map() is how every traversal in pidly works — list, tree, check, new, reap,
init, and anything 25 or 27 adds — and it is written as:

  find "$1" -mindepth 1 -type d '!' -name c '!' -name '.*' -printf '%f %p\n'

-printf is a GNU extension. BSD find, which is what macOS ships, does not
have it. So on the platform Homebrew exists to serve, every one of those
commands fails, and 29 cannot ship until this is fixed.

This has never been noticed because the machine pidly was written on has
bfs 4.1.1 as find, which implements the GNU options. Checked 2026-09-17:
find --version reports bfs, not GNU findutils, and the format's own
documents assume nothing more than POSIX while the script quietly does not.

A candidate replacement, verified byte-identical on this tracker's 40
tickets on 2026-09-17:

  find "$1" -mindepth 1 -type d '!' -name c '!' -name '.*' -print |
      awk -F/ '{ print $NF" "$0 }'

-mindepth is BSD as well as GNU, so it stays. The basename moves into the
awk that every caller already pipes through. Verify on a real macOS find
rather than on bfs, and check the rest of the script for the same habit
while there — the reference and AGENTS.md publish find idioms that agents
will run, so those have to be portable too, and they are the ones nobody
tests.

Worth deciding at the same time: whether the test suite can assert this at
all. A suite running on one machine cannot prove portability, but it can
refuse the specific extensions — a grep over bin/pidly for -printf and
friends is a cheap guard against the habit coming back.

Done when map and every other traversal run under a POSIX find, verified on
macOS, the published idioms are portable too, and something in the suite
notices if a GNU-only option reappears.
